Date:     Thu, 20 May 93 7:32:09 GMT
From: starr@genie.slhs.udel.edu
Subject: More About the FDA

In support of Phil Fraering: when Mary Ruwart spoke at an ISIL reception at
Lehr's Greenhouse here in San Francisco, she made two claims that undercut
the case for the FDA strongly.

1) Before the FDA, there were two private testing agencies for medications:
the AMA did its own testing, as did a consumer group.

2) The FDA doesn't do most of the testing it's involved with.  It mandates
that the testing be done by the industries within the scope of its powers.

In other words, instead of having two competing disinterested parties to
the testing of medications, we now have most of the testing being done by
parties with an interest in findings that support the safety of their product
- all under the gun of the FDA, which files the studies, but does little else
with them, such as check to see if they can be replicated.

As if that wasn't enough, neither aspirin nor penicillin could pass FDA
requirements today.  The mandated tests have little to do with the safety
of the medications in humans, and a lot to do with covering bureaucratic
behinds.

ISIL, of course, sells an issue paper on the FDA titled "Death By Regulation"
that was written by Dr. Ruwart (former surgery professor and currently
research scientist at Upjohn Pharmeceutical).  She also holds a patent on
a product that would prevent much liver disease if it could be put into
alcoholic beverages, but the FDA won't permit her to do that - at the cost
of about 10,000 lives per year (Gross Domestic Body Count - GDBC) as well
as profit to her and her firm.

