Subject: Gold -- Clinton as crypto-Randian 

Dan Davis wrote:
 
  Second, with all due respect, government decisions to buy or sell
  effect market demand and supply, and so effect price.

Harry Browne has made the observation that, with gold, changes in
demand have a much stronger effect on the price than changes in
supply. Increases in demand generally come from individuals throughout
the world who hold a reserve stock of dollars to protect against
inflation in their local currencies. When these people fear inflation
in the dollar, they move part of their reserve savings from dollars
into gold. Personally, I think if all the small dollar-holders all
over the world become convinced that the Clinton administration will
bring dollar inflation back, and if May's inflation numbers look
anything like April's, the world governments can sell all they want
and they won't keep the price of gold from being set primarily by the
demand of individuals.

