On some points on the Lasagne Test:

derek: How does this help?  Turing's test is at least clear-cut.  In order
   to make this work, we need to define desire -- is this lots simpler
   than defining intelligence?

Weeeeelllllll, Turing's is fairly clear-cut, but doesn't measure anything
that seems _useful_ (to me). This one _is_ more fuzzy, but if you can get
a consensus of `yeah, it seems to pass', you've got a _useful_ machine. 
Very useful, in fact: a machine that general-purpose should be able to
eliminate 90% of known work [1] which will be a Good Thing[2]. Could we
make it more precise? Then it becomes more like a standard computer
benchmark: you produce a machine that does _that one_ very well, and
everyone claims it's the best... I suspect the Lasagne Test _is_ probably
more useful as a subjective rule-of-thumb, and anyway, tightening up the
notion of desire could result in some undesireable side-effects[3].


As to the formal description, yeah, it's a bit crap. Reads thus (to me):
for a given person, and a desire of theirs, the machine is intelligent if
it's possible to deduce the desire from knowing about the person, and the
machine does so, on average, more often than the Average Man [4]

