From: derek@cs.wisc.edu (Derek Zahn)
Subject: Chaitin, Randomness, and Explanations on this List

Dan Davis asks:

> What is the random process generating the turing machines?

Coin flips, whatever.

> What distribution of turing machines are we sampling from?

This is a good question -- after all, a turing machine
could have an arbitrarily long specification.  The answer
is that to get around this, Omega is actually the halting
probability if n-bit programs have measure 2^{-n} -- so
apparently the distribution is exponentially weighted toward
shorter turing machines.  This fact is crucial in the technique
to show that omega could be used to "solve" the halting problem
if we knew its value.

derek


